10078028|t|Lithium-induced nephrogenic diabetes insipidus: changes in the osteopontin synthesis and urinary excretion.
10078028|a|Lithium (Li) is widely used for psychiatric disorders, but its prolonged administration frequently causes nephrogenic diabetes insipidus (NDI). In this study, adult male Wistar rats were treated with lithium chloride for 4 weeks. Lithium treatment induced severe polyuria, polydipsia, and impaired urinary concentrating capacity. Renal biopsy revealed marked tubular lesions in the collecting ducts. Our findings demonstrate that lithium directly causes nephrogenic diabetes insipidus accompanied by prominent tubular injury.
